 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The flower is the organ of sexual reproduction of flowering plants. Its parts, from outside to inside:\n• sepals (green): protect the flower bud; together they form the calyx;\n• petals (often coloured and scented): attract insects; together they form the corolla;\n• stamens, the male parts: each has a filament holding up an anther, which makes pollen grains (male sex cells);\n• carpels (pistil), the female part: the stigma receives pollen, the style joins it to the ovary, and the ovary contains ovules (with the female sex cells).\nAll these parts sit on the receptacle, at the top of the flower stalk.\nA flower with both stamens and carpels is bisexual (hibiscus); some flowers have only one sex (maize has separate male and female flowers).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "k": 4057
  },
  "problem": "In the drawing, the anther is labelled {bioLetter(k, 8, 2)}. What does it do, and which part holds it up?",
  "steps": [
   "The anther is the top of the stamen: it makes pollen grains, the male sex cells.",
   "It is held up by the filament, labelled {bioLetter(k, 8, 3)}."
  ],
  "answer": "It makes pollen; the filament ({bioLetter(k, 8, 3)}) holds it up.",
  "checks": [],
  "figure": "flower(k)"
 },
